KORPIS LICENSING
================

Copyright the Korpis contributors.

Korpis is not licensed under a single licence. It is split by layer, deliberately,
for the reasons argued in section 5 of docs/00-overview.md and restated in section
2 of docs/19-governance.md.

  Protocol definitions, client SDKs, recipe format, agent protocol,
  conformance suites
      Apache-2.0                            full text in LICENSE-APACHE

  Control plane, node agent, relay, web / CLI / chat clients
      AGPL-3.0-or-later                     full text in LICENSE-AGPL

  The documents in this repository (docs/, README, and the other
  Markdown files)
      CC BY 4.0                             full text in LICENSE-CC-BY

  Recipes, extensions, themes
      the author's choice, proprietary included

WHY THE SPLIT

Anything a third party must implement to interoperate (the wire protocol, the recipe
format, the agent contract) is Apache-2.0, because Rule K-7 requires that someone
else be able to write a conforming agent without friction, and a copyleft protocol
definition would defeat that. So are the conformance suites that say what conforming
means, which a third party runs against what it wrote. Apache-2.0 also carries an explicit patent grant. The
platform itself is AGPL, because running it for other people is exactly the case
AGPL exists to cover.

Extensions are separate processes speaking a published contract, not derivative
works. They may be licensed however their authors wish, including proprietary and
paid.

WHAT THIS MEANS IF YOU RUN IT

Stated plainly, because AGPL is routinely misread as a restriction on use:

    Run it commercially. Sell capacity on it. Charge whatever you like.
    Build closed extensions against it. Running it, unmodified, for your
    customers obliges you to nothing.

Two things do create obligations. If you modify Korpis itself and let others use
the modified version over a network, you offer those users the source of your
version (AGPL section 13). If you convey copies, which includes distributing
binaries or the CLI and serving the web client's JavaScript to a browser, the
licence and the corresponding source go with them (AGPL sections 4 to 6); every
Korpis build names the exact source revision it was built from, so an unmodified
deployment meets this as shipped. There is no per-workload, per-node,
per-instance, per-account or per-domain charge, at any scale, ever.

CONTRIBUTIONS

Contributions are accepted under the Developer Certificate of Origin. Sign off with
"git commit -s". There is no contributor licence agreement and no copyright
assignment, which keeps copyright distributed: as other people's contributions
accumulate, relicensing their code requires their consent. What the licence does
not do is prevent new proprietary components from being added beside the open
core; that is prevented by the project's policy (Principle P10 and Rule K-13), a
public promise rather than a legal mechanism. Section 2 of docs/19-governance.md
states both halves. See CONTRIBUTING.md.

DOCUMENTATION

The documents in this repository are the specification. Until the first code
release they are the whole of the work, and they are published under CC BY 4.0 so
that they can be quoted, argued with, and built on freely. Attribution is the only
condition. The full licence text is in LICENSE-CC-BY.

TRADEMARK

The Korpis name and marks are held separately from the code and are not licensed by
the above. Unmodified builds may use them freely, and commercial hosting under the
Korpis name is explicitly permitted. A modified distribution must be renamed. Not to
discourage forks, but so that a user who downloads something called Korpis gets
Korpis and security advisories refer to a knowable artifact. See section 3 of
docs/19-governance.md.
